          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My mother has heart failure.",
          "answer": "Heart failure is a long-term condition in which the heart cannot pump or fill with blood as well as the body needs. The name can sound like the heart has stopped, but it has not. Many people live with it for years with treatment (condition-heart-failure).\n\n**How it is classified:** Doctors often sort it by ejection fraction, the share of blood the left side of the heart pushes out with each beat (condition-heart-failure; guideline-heart-failure-reduced-ef-therapy):\n- **Reduced:** 40% or below\n- **Mildly reduced:** 41% to 49%\n- **Preserved:** 50% or above\n\nA preserved result is not a milder form. It is a different problem, usually with a stiffer heart that fills poorly. For medical records, heart failure is coded I50 in ICD-10-CM, and the code can also describe the type and whether it is acute, chronic, or both (coding-heart-failure-i50).\n\n**What caregivers may need to know:** The passages I could retrieve mention a daily weight check. A sudden rise in weight can be a sign that fluid is building up, and it is a common reason to call the care team early. Many people are given a written action plan listing the warning signs and what to do at each level. If your mother has one, that plan should guide what you do, more than general information. I could not retrieve passages on other caregiver topics, such as symptoms to watch for or how to support daily routines, so I can't cover those from my sources.\n\nIf your mother has sudden severe breathlessness, chest pain, or fainting, call emergency services right away.\n\nWhat would you like to learn or do next? For example, I could look up lifestyle guidance on diet or fluids, explain the reduced and preserved types in more detail, or go over what to ask her care team.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
