          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does she have to weigh herself every day?",
          "answer": "Daily weighing is a common self-check, but the reason depends on the condition. The passages I found cover only one of the reasons you listed, heart failure. I could not find passages on daily weighing for kidney or liver conditions or for other treatment plans, so I can't explain those from my sources.\n\n**In heart failure:** Weight is used as a rough measure of fluid. The body can hold extra fluid when the heart is not pumping well. Fluid can build up before swelling or breathlessness shows, and weight can rise faster than those symptoms appear. A gain of a few pounds over two or three days, or a larger gain over a week, is a standard reason to contact the care team. Weigh yourself at the same time each day, after using the bathroom and before eating, using the same scale, and write the result down so it can be compared across days (lifestyle-heart-failure-diet; condition-heart-failure).\n\n**Your mother's plan:** Many people are given a written action plan that lists the weight change that should prompt a call and what to do at each level. If she has one, follow that plan and ask her care team about any numbers that are unclear.\n\nPlease ask her prescribing clinician why daily weighing was recommended for her specifically, and what weight change they want her to report. The answer depends on her diagnosis and treatment.\n\nIf she has sudden severe breathlessness, chest pain, or fainting, call emergency services right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n[sources: condition-heart-failure, lifestyle-heart-failure-diet, guideline-heart-failure-reduced-ef-therapy, coding-heart-failure-i50]",
