          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What about salt?",
          "answer": "Salt is mostly sodium. Sodium is the part of salt that affects the body, so health guidance usually talks about sodium. (lifestyle-hypertension-diet)\n\n**Why it matters:** Sodium affects blood pressure. Lower intake is linked to lower blood pressure, and any reduction helps, even if a target is not reached. In heart failure, sodium holds water in the body, so intake is also tied to fluid build-up (lifestyle-hypertension-diet; lifestyle-heart-failure-diet).\n\n[removed: this system does not give doses or amounts.] These are general figures, not personal advice (lifestyle-hypertension-diet).\n\n**Where it comes from:** Most dietary sodium is not from the salt shaker. It is mainly in packaged and restaurant foods such as bread, processed meat, cheese, sauces, soups, snacks and prepared meals. Reading sodium on food labels is described as more useful than any single swap (lifestyle-hypertension-diet).\n\n**Could you clarify what you mean?** Are you asking about:\n1. Salt in your diet, or in a particular food?\n2. Salt and a specific condition, such as high blood pressure or heart failure?\n3. A symptom, such as swelling or thirst?\n4. A product, such as a salt substitute?\n\nFor heart failure, the right salt or fluid limit is individual. Please ask your mother's care team for her own target.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n[sources: lifestyle-hypertension-diet, lifestyle-heart-failure-diet, guideline-heart-failure-reduced-ef-therapy, condition-heart-failure]",
